<body>
<p align=3D"CENTER">
<b>BERKSHIRE HATHAWAY INC.</b>
</p>

<pre>

<b>To the Shareholders of Berkshire Hathaway Inc.:</b>


     Our gain in net worth during 1994 was $1.45 billion or 13.9%. =20
Over the last 30 years (that is, since present management took=20
over) our per-share book value has grown from $19 to $10,083, or=20
at a rate of 23% compounded annually.

     Charlie Munger, Berkshire's Vice Chairman and my partner,=20
and I make few predictions.  One we will confidently offer,=20
however, is that the future performance of Berkshire won't come=20
close to matching the performance of the past.

     The problem is not that what has worked in the past will=20
cease to work in the future.  To the contrary, we believe that=20
our formula - the purchase at sensible prices of businesses that=20
have good underlying economics and are run by honest and able=20
people - is certain to produce reasonable success.  We expect,=20
therefore, to keep on doing well.

     A fat wallet, however, is the enemy of superior investment=20
results.  And Berkshire now has a net worth of $11.9 billion=20
compared to about $22 million when Charlie and I began to manage=20
the company.  Though there are as many good businesses as ever,=20
it is useless for us to make purchases that are inconsequential=20
in relation to Berkshire's capital.  (As Charlie regularly=20
reminds me, "If something is not worth doing at all, it's not=20
worth doing well.")  We now consider a security for purchase only=20
if we believe we can deploy at least $100 million in it.  Given=20
that minimum, Berkshire's investment universe has shrunk=20
dramatically.

     Nevertheless, we will stick with the approach that got us=20
here and try not to relax our standards.  Ted Williams, in=20
<u>The Story of My Life</u>, explains why:  "My argument is, to be=20
a good hitter, you've got to get a good ball to hit.  It's the=20
first rule in the book.  If I have to bite at stuff that is out=20
of my happy zone, I'm not a .344 hitter.  I might only be a .250=20
hitter."  Charlie and I agree and will try to wait for=20
opportunities that are well within our own "happy zone."

     We will continue to ignore political and economic forecasts,=20
which are an expensive distraction for many investors and=20
businessmen.  Thirty years ago, no one could have foreseen the=20
huge expansion of the Vietnam War, wage and price controls, two=20
oil shocks, the resignation of a president, the dissolution of=20
the Soviet Union, a one-day drop in the Dow of 508 points, or=20
treasury bill yields fluctuating between 2.8% and 17.4%.

     But, surprise - none of these blockbuster events made the=20
slightest dent in Ben Graham's investment principles.  Nor did=20
they render unsound the negotiated purchases of fine businesses=20
at sensible prices.  Imagine the cost to us, then, if we had let=20
a fear of unknowns cause us to defer or alter the deployment of=20
capital.  Indeed, we have usually made our best purchases when=20
apprehensions about some macro event were at a peak.  Fear is the=20
foe of the faddist, but the friend of the fundamentalist.

     A different set of major shocks is sure to occur in the next=20
30 years.  We will neither try to predict these nor to profit=20
from them.  If we can identify businesses similar to those we=20
have purchased in the past, external surprises will have little=20
effect on our long-term results.

     What we promise you - along with more modest gains - is that=20
during your ownership of Berkshire, you will fare just as Charlie=20
and I do.  If you suffer, we will suffer; if we prosper, so will=20
you.  And we will not break this bond by introducing compensation=20
arrangements that give us a greater participation in the upside=20
than the downside.

     We further promise you that our personal fortunes will=20
remain overwhelmingly concentrated in Berkshire shares:  We will=20
not ask you to invest with us and then put our own money=20
elsewhere.  In addition, Berkshire dominates both the investment=20
portfolios of most members of our families and of a great many=20
friends who belonged to partnerships that Charlie and I ran in=20
the 1960's.  We could not be more motivated to do our best.

     Luckily, we have a good base from which to work.  Ten years=20
ago, in 1984, Berkshire's insurance companies held securities=20
having a value of $1.7 billion, or about $1,500 per Berkshire=20
share.  Leaving aside all income and capital gains from those=20
securities, Berkshire's pre-tax earnings that year were only=20
about $6 million.  We had earnings, yes, from our various=20
manufacturing, retailing and service businesses, but they were=20
almost entirely offset by the combination of underwriting losses=20
in our insurance business, corporate overhead and interest=20
expense.

     Now we hold securities worth $18 billion, or over $15,000=20
per Berkshire share.  If you again exclude all income from these=20
securities, our pre-tax earnings in 1994 were about $384 million.=20
During the decade, employment has grown from 5,000 to 22,000=20
(including eleven people at World Headquarters).

     We achieved our gains through the efforts of a superb corps=20
of operating managers who get extraordinary results from some=20
ordinary-appearing businesses.  Casey Stengel described managing=20
a baseball team as "getting paid for home runs other fellows=20
hit."  That's my formula at Berkshire, also.

     The businesses in which we have partial interests are=20
equally important to Berkshire's success.  A few statistics will=20
illustrate their significance:  In 1994, Coca-Cola sold about 280=20
billion 8-ounce servings and earned a little less than a penny on=20
each.  But pennies add up.  Through Berkshire's 7.8% ownership of=20
Coke, we have an economic interest in 21 billion of its servings,=20
which produce "soft-drink earnings" for us of nearly $200=20
million.  Similarly, by way of its Gillette stock, Berkshire has=20
a 7% share of the world's razor and blade market (measured by=20
revenues, not by units), a proportion according us about $250=20
million of sales in 1994.  And, at Wells Fargo, a $53 billion=20
bank, our 13% ownership translates into a $7 billion "Berkshire=20
Bank" that earned about $100 million during 1994.

     It's far better to own a significant portion of the Hope=20
diamond than 100% of a rhinestone, and the companies just=20
mentioned easily qualify as rare gems.  Best of all, we aren't=20
limited to simply a few of this breed, but instead possess a=20
growing collection.

     Stock prices will continue to fluctuate - sometimes sharply=20
- and the economy will have its ups and down.  Over time,=20
however, we believe it highly probable that the sort of=20
businesses we own will continue to increase in value at a=20
satisfactory rate.


<b>Book Value and Intrinsic Value</b>

     We regularly report our per-share book value, an easily=20
calculable number, though one of limited use.  Just as regularly,=20
we tell you that what counts is intrinsic value, a number that is=20
impossible to pinpoint but essential to estimate.

     For example, in 1964, we could state with certitude that=20
Berkshire's per-share book value was $19.46.  However, that=20
figure considerably overstated the stock's intrinsic value since=20
all of the company's resources were tied up in a sub-profitable=20
textile business.  Our textile assets had neither going-concern=20
nor liquidation values equal to their carrying values.  In 1964,=20
then, anyone inquiring into the soundness of Berkshire's balance=20
sheet might well have deserved the answer once offered up by a=20
Hollywood mogul of dubious reputation:  "Don't worry, the=20
liabilities are solid."

     Today, Berkshire's situation has reversed:  Many of the=20
businesses we control are worth far more than their carrying=20
value.  (Those we don't control, such as Coca-Cola or Gillette,=20
are carried at current market values.)  We continue to give you=20
book value figures, however, because they serve as a rough,=20
albeit significantly understated, tracking measure for Berkshire's=20
intrinsic value.  Last year, in fact, the two measures moved in=20
concert:  Book value gained 13.9%, and that was the approximate=20
gain in intrinsic value also.

     We define intrinsic value as the discounted value of the=20
cash that can be taken out of a business during its remaining=20
life.  Anyone calculating intrinsic value necessarily comes up=20
with a highly subjective figure that will change both as=20
estimates of future cash flows are revised and as interest rates=20
move.  Despite its fuzziness, however, intrinsic value is all-
important and is the only logical way to evaluate the relative=20
attractiveness of investments and businesses.

     To see how historical input (book value) and future output=20
(intrinsic value) can diverge, let's look at another form of=20
investment, a college education.  Think of the education's cost=20
as its "book value."  If it is to be accurate, the cost should=20
include the earnings that were foregone by the student because he=20
chose college rather than a job.

     For this exercise, we will ignore the important non-economic=20
benefits of an education and focus strictly on its economic=20
value.  First, we must estimate the earnings that the graduate=20
will receive over his lifetime and subtract from that figure an=20
estimate of what he would have earned had he lacked his=20
education.  That gives us an excess earnings figure, which must=20
then be discounted, at an appropriate interest rate, back to=20
graduation day.  The dollar result equals the intrinsic economic=20
value of the education.

     Some graduates will find that the book value of their=20
education exceeds its intrinsic value, which means that whoever=20
paid for the education didn't get his money's worth.  In other=20
cases, the intrinsic value of an education will far exceed its=20
book value, a result that proves capital was wisely deployed.  In=20
all cases, what is clear is that book value is meaningless as an=20
indicator of intrinsic value.

     Now let's get less academic and look at Scott Fetzer, an=20
example from Berkshire's own experience.  This account will not=20
only illustrate how the relationship of book value and intrinsic=20
value can change but also will provide an accounting lesson that=20
I know you have been breathlessly awaiting.  Naturally, I've=20
chosen here to talk about an acquisition that has turned out to=20
be a huge winner.

     Berkshire purchased Scott Fetzer at the beginning of 1986. =20
At the time, the company was a collection of 22 businesses, and=20
today we have exactly the same line-up - no additions and no=20
disposals.  Scott Fetzer's main operations are World Book, Kirby,=20
and Campbell Hausfeld, but many other units are important=20
contributors to earnings as well.

     We paid $315.2 million for Scott Fetzer, which at the time=20
had $172.6 million of book value.  The $142.6 million premium we=20
handed over indicated our belief that the company's intrinsic=20
value was close to double its book value.

     In the table below we trace the book value of Scott Fetzer,=20
as well as its earnings and dividends, since our purchase.
