     Because it had excess cash when our deal was made, Scott=20
Fetzer was able to pay Berkshire dividends of $125 million in=20
1986, though it earned only $40.3 million.  I should mention that=20
we have not introduced leverage into Scott Fetzer's balance=20
sheet.  In fact, the company has gone from very modest debt when=20
we purchased it to virtually no debt at all (except for debt used=20
by its finance subsidiary).  Similarly, we have not sold plants=20
and leased them back, nor sold receivables, nor the like. =20
Throughout our years of ownership, Scott Fetzer has operated as a=20
conservatively-financed and liquid enterprise.

     As you can see, Scott Fetzer's earnings have increased=20
steadily since we bought it, but book value has not grown=20
commensurately.  Consequently, return on equity, which was=20
exceptional at the time of our purchase, has now become truly=20
extraordinary.  Just how extraordinary is illustrated by=20
comparing Scott Fetzer's performance to that of the Fortune 500,=20
a group it would qualify for if it were a stand-alone company.

     Had Scott Fetzer been on the 1993 500 list - the latest=20
available for inspection - the company's return on equity would=20
have ranked 4th.  But that is far from the whole story.  The top=20
three companies in return on equity were Insilco, LTV and Gaylord=20
Container, each of which emerged from bankruptcy in 1993 and none=20
of which achieved meaningful earnings that year except for those=20
they realized when they were accorded debt forgiveness in=20
bankruptcy proceedings.  Leaving aside such non-operating=20
windfalls, Scott Fetzer's return on equity would have ranked it=20
first on the Fortune 500, well ahead of number two.  Indeed,=20
Scott Fetzer's return on equity was double that of the company=20
ranking tenth.

     You might expect that Scott Fetzer's success could only be=20
explained by a cyclical peak in earnings, a monopolistic=20
position, or leverage.  But no such circumstances apply.  Rather,=20
the company's success comes from the managerial expertise of CEO=20
Ralph Schey, of whom I'll tell you more later.

     First, however, the promised accounting lesson:  When we=20
paid a $142.6 million premium over book value for Scott Fetzer,=20
that figure had to be recorded on Berkshire's balance sheet. =20
I'll spare you the details of how this worked (these were laid=20
out in an appendix to our 1986 Annual Report) and get to the=20
bottom line:  After a premium is initially recorded, it must in=20
almost all cases be written off over time through annual charges=20
that are shown as costs in the acquiring company's earnings=20
statement.

     The following table shows, first, the annual charges=20
Berkshire has made to gradually extinguish the Scott Fetzer=20
acquisition premium and, second, the premium that remains on our=20
books.  These charges have no effect on cash or the taxes we pay,=20
and are not, in our view, an economic cost (though many=20
accountants would disagree with us).  They are merely a way for=20
us to reduce the carrying value of Scott Fetzer on our books so=20
that the figure will eventually match the net worth that Scott=20
Fetzer actually employs in its business.
