     Note that by the end of 1994 the premium was reduced to=20
$54.2 million.  When this figure is added to Scott Fetzer's year-
end book value of $94 million, the total is $148.2 million, which=20
is the current carrying value of Scott Fetzer on Berkshire's=20
books.  That amount is less than half of our carrying value for=20
the company when it was acquired.  Yet Scott Fetzer is now=20
earning about twice what it then did.  Clearly, the intrinsic=20
value of the business has consistently grown, even though we have=20
just as consistently marked down its carrying value through=20
purchase-premium charges that reduced Berkshire's earnings and=20
net worth.

     The difference between Scott Fetzer's intrinsic value and=20
its carrying value on Berkshire's books is now huge.  As I=20
mentioned earlier - but am delighted to mention again - credit=20
for this agreeable mismatch goes to Ralph Schey, a focused, smart=20
and high-grade manager.

     The reasons for Ralph's success are not complicated.  Ben=20
Graham taught me 45 years ago that in investing it is not=20
necessary to do extraordinary things to get extraordinary=20
results.  In later life, I have been surprised to find that this=20
statement holds true in business management as well.  What a=20
manager must do is handle the basics well and not get diverted. =20
That's precisely Ralph's formula.  He establishes the right goals=20
and never forgets what he set out to do.  On the personal side,=20
Ralph is a joy to work with.  He's forthright about problems and=20
is self-confident without being self-important.

     He is also experienced.  Though I don't know Ralph's age, I=20
do know that, like many of our managers, he is over 65.  At=20
Berkshire, we look to performance, not to the calendar.  Charlie=20
and I, at 71 and 64 respectively, now keep George Foreman's=20
picture on our desks.  You can make book that our scorn for a=20
mandatory retirement age will grow stronger every year.


<b>Intrinsic Value and Capital Allocation</b>

     Understanding intrinsic value is as important for managers=20
as it is for investors.  When managers are making capital=20
allocation decisions - including decisions to repurchase shares -=20
it's vital that they act in ways that increase per-share=20
intrinsic value and avoid moves that decrease it.  This principle=20
may seem obvious but we constantly see it violated.  And, when=20
misallocations occur, shareholders are hurt.

     For example, in contemplating business mergers and=20
acquisitions, many managers tend to focus on whether the=20
transaction is immediately dilutive or anti-dilutive to earnings=20
per share (or, at financial institutions, to per-share book=20
value).  An emphasis of this sort carries great dangers.  Going=20
back to our college-education example, imagine that a 25-year-old=20
first-year MBA student is considering merging his future economic=20
interests with those of a 25-year-old day laborer.  The MBA=20
student, a non-earner, would find that a "share-for-share" merger=20
of his equity interest in himself with that of the day laborer=20
would enhance his near-term earnings (in a big way!).  But what=20
could be sillier for the student than a deal of this kind?

     In corporate transactions, it's equally silly for the would-
be purchaser to focus on current earnings when the prospective=20
acquiree has either different prospects, different amounts of=20
non-operating assets, or a different capital structure.  At=20
Berkshire, we have rejected many merger and purchase=20
opportunities that would have boosted current and near-term=20
earnings but that would have reduced per-share intrinsic value. =20
Our approach, rather, has been to follow Wayne Gretzky's advice:=20
"Go to where the puck is going to be, not to where it is."  As a=20
result, our shareholders are now many billions of dollars richer=20
than they would have been if we had used the standard catechism.

     The sad fact is that most major acquisitions display an=20
egregious imbalance:  They are a bonanza for the shareholders of=20
the acquiree; they increase the income and status of the=20
acquirer's management; and they are a honey pot for the=20
investment bankers and other professionals on both sides.  But,=20
alas, they usually reduce the wealth of the acquirer's shareholders,=20
often to a substantial extent.  That happens because the acquirer=20
typically gives up more intrinsic value than it receives.  Do that=20
enough, says John Medlin, the retired head of Wachovia Corp., and=20
"you are running a chain letter in reverse."

     Over time, the skill with which a company's managers=20
allocate capital has an enormous impact on the enterprise's=20
value.  Almost by definition, a really good business generates=20
far more money (at least after its early years) than it can use=20
internally.  The company could, of course, distribute the money=20
to shareholders by way of dividends or share repurchases.  But=20
often the CEO asks a strategic planning staff, consultants or=20
investment bankers whether an acquisition or two might make=20
sense.  That's like asking your interior decorator whether you=20
need a $50,000 rug.

     The acquisition problem is often compounded by a biological=20
bias:  Many CEO's attain their positions in part because they=20
possess an abundance of animal spirits and ego.  If an executive=20
is heavily endowed with these qualities - which, it should be=20
acknowledged, sometimes have their advantages - they won't=20
disappear when he reaches the top.  When such a CEO is encouraged=20
by his advisors to make deals, he responds much as would a=20
teenage boy who is encouraged by his father to have a normal sex=20
life.  It's not a push he needs.

     Some years back, a CEO friend of mine - in jest, it must be=20
said - unintentionally described the pathology of many big deals.=20
This friend, who ran a property-casualty insurer, was explaining=20
to his directors why he wanted to acquire a certain life=20
insurance company.  After droning rather unpersuasively through=20
the economics and strategic rationale for the acquisition, he=20
abruptly abandoned the script.  With an impish look, he simply=20
said:  "Aw, fellas, all the other kids have one."

     At Berkshire, our managers will continue to earn=20
extraordinary returns from what appear to be ordinary businesses.=20
As a first step, these managers will look for ways to deploy=20
their earnings advantageously in their businesses.  What's left,=20
they will send to Charlie and me.  We then will try to use those=20
funds in ways that build per-share intrinsic value.  Our goal=20
will be to acquire either part or all of businesses that we=20
believe we understand, that have good, sustainable underlying=20
economics, and that are run by managers whom we like, admire and=20
trust.


<b>Compensation</b>

     At Berkshire, we try to be as logical about compensation as=20
about capital allocation.  For example, we compensate Ralph Schey=20
based upon the results of Scott Fetzer rather than those of=20
Berkshire.  What could make more sense, since he's responsible=20
for one operation but not the other?  A cash bonus or a stock=20
option tied to the fortunes of Berkshire would provide totally=20
capricious rewards to Ralph.  He could, for example, be hitting=20
home runs at Scott Fetzer while Charlie and I rang up mistakes at=20
Berkshire, thereby negating his efforts many times over. =20
Conversely, why should option profits or bonuses be heaped upon=20
Ralph if good things are occurring in other parts of Berkshire=20
but Scott Fetzer is lagging?

     In setting compensation, we like to hold out the promise of=20
large carrots, but make sure their delivery is tied directly to=20
results in the area that a manager controls.  When capital=20
invested in an operation is significant, we also both charge=20
managers a high rate for incremental capital they employ and=20
credit them at an equally high rate for capital they release.

     The product of this money's-not-free approach is definitely=20
visible at Scott Fetzer.  If Ralph can employ incremental funds=20
at good returns, it pays him to do so:  His bonus increases when=20
earnings on additional capital exceed a meaningful hurdle charge.=20
But our bonus calculation is symmetrical:  If incremental=20
investment yields sub-standard returns, the shortfall is costly=20
to Ralph as well as to Berkshire.  The consequence of this two-
way arrangement is that it pays Ralph - and pays him well - to=20
send to Omaha any cash he can't advantageously use in his=20
business.

     It has become fashionable at public companies to describe=20
almost every compensation plan as aligning the interests of=20
management with those of shareholders.  In our book, alignment=20
means being a partner in both directions, not just on the upside.=20
Many "alignment" plans flunk this basic test, being artful forms=20
of "heads I win, tails you lose."

     A common form of misalignment occurs in the typical stock=20
option arrangement, which does not periodically increase the=20
option price to compensate for the fact that retained earnings=20
are building up the wealth of the company.  Indeed, the=20
combination of a ten-year option, a low dividend payout, and=20
compound interest can provide lush gains to a manager who has=20
done no more than tread water in his job.  A cynic might even=20
note that when payments to owners are held down, the profit to=20
the option-holding manager increases.  I have yet to see this=20
vital point spelled out in a proxy statement asking shareholders=20
to approve an option plan.

     I can't resist mentioning that our compensation arrangement=20
with Ralph Schey was worked out in about five minutes,=20
immediately upon our purchase of Scott Fetzer and without the=20
"help" of lawyers or compensation consultants.  This arrangement=20
embodies a few very simple ideas - not the kind of terms favored=20
by consultants who cannot easily send a large bill unless they=20
have established that you have a large problem (and one, of=20
course, that requires an annual review).  Our agreement with=20
Ralph has never been changed.  It made sense to him and to me in=20
1986, and it makes sense now.  Our compensation arrangements with=20
the managers of all our other units are similarly simple, though=20
the terms of each agreement vary to fit the economic=20
characteristics of the business at issue, the existence in some=20
cases of partial ownership of the unit by managers, etc.

     In all instances, we pursue rationality.  Arrangements that=20
pay off in capricious ways, unrelated to a manager's personal=20
accomplishments, may well be welcomed by certain managers.  Who,=20
after all, refuses a free lottery ticket?  But such arrangements=20
are wasteful to the company and cause the manager to lose focus=20
on what should be his real areas of concern.  Additionally,=20
irrational behavior at the parent may well encourage imitative=20
behavior at subsidiaries.

     At Berkshire, only Charlie and I have the managerial=20
responsibility for the entire business.  Therefore, we are the=20
only parties who should logically be compensated on the basis of=20
what the enterprise does as a whole.  Even so, that is not a=20
compensation arrangement we desire.  We have carefully designed=20
both the company and our jobs so that we do things we enjoy with=20
people we like.  Equally important, we are forced to do very few=20
boring or unpleasant tasks.  We are the beneficiaries as well of=20
the abundant array of material and psychic perks that flow to the=20
heads of corporations.  Under such idyllic conditions, we don't=20
expect shareholders to ante up loads of compensation for which we=20
have no possible need.

     Indeed, if we were not paid at all, Charlie and I would be=20
delighted with the cushy jobs we hold.  At bottom, we subscribe=20
to Ronald Reagan's creed:  "It's probably true that hard work=20
never killed anyone, but I figure why take the chance."


<b>Sources of Reported Earnings</b>

     The table on the next page shows the main sources of=20
Berkshire's reported earnings.  In this presentation, purchase-
premium charges of the type we discussed in our earlier analysis=20
of Scott Fetzer are not assigned to the specific businesses to=20
