<b>"Look-Through" Earnings</b>

     In past reports, we've discussed look-through earnings, which=20
we believe more accurately portray the earnings of Berkshire than=20
does our GAAP result.  As we calculate them, look-through earnings=20
consist of: (1) the operating earnings reported in the previous=20
section, plus; (2) the retained operating earnings of major=20
investees that, under GAAP accounting, are not reflected in our=20
profits, less; (3) an allowance for the tax that would be paid by=20
Berkshire if these retained earnings of investees had instead been=20
distributed to us.  The "operating earnings" of which we speak here=20
exclude capital gains, special accounting items and major=20
restructuring charges.

     If our intrinsic value is to grow at our target rate of 15%,=20
our look-through earnings, over time, must also increase at about=20
that pace.  When I first explained this concept a few years back, I=20
told you that meeting this 15% goal would require us to generate=20
look-through earnings of about $1.8 billion by 2000.  Because we've=20
since issued about 3% more shares, that figure has grown to $1.85=20
billion.

     We are now modestly ahead of schedule in meeting our goal, but=20
to a significant degree that is because our super-cat insurance=20
business has recently delivered earnings far above trend-line=20
expectancy (an outcome I will discuss in the next section).  Giving=20
due weight to that abnormality, we still expect to hit our target=20
but that, of course, is no sure thing.

