<b>Insurance Operations</b>

     As we've explained in past reports, what counts in our=20
insurance business is, first, the amount of "float" we develop and,=20
second, its cost to us.  Float is money we hold but don't own.  In=20
an insurance operation, float arises because most policies require=20
that premiums be prepaid and, more importantly, because it usually=20
takes time for an insurer to hear about and resolve loss claims.

     Typically, the premiums that an insurer takes in do not cover=20
the losses and expenses it must pay.  That leaves it running an=20
"underwriting loss" - and that loss is the cost of float.

     An insurance business is profitable over time if its cost of=20
float is less than the cost the company would otherwise incur to=20
obtain funds.  But the business has a negative value if the cost of=20
its float is higher than market rates for money.

     As the numbers in the following table show, Berkshire's=20
insurance business has been an enormous winner.  For the table, we=20
have compiled our float -  which we generate in exceptional amounts=20
relative to our premium volume - by adding loss reserves, loss=20
adjustment reserves, funds held under reinsurance assumed and unearned=20
premium reserves and then subtracting agents' balances, prepaid=20
acquisition costs, prepaid taxes and deferred charges applicable to=20
assumed reinsurance.  Our cost of float is determined by our=20
underwriting loss or profit.  In those years when we have had an=20
underwriting profit, such as the last two, our cost of float has been=20
negative, and we have determined our insurance earnings by adding=20
underwriting profit to float income.

