     Charlie and I are delighted that our float grew in 1994 and=20
are even more pleased that it proved to be cost-free.  But our=20
message this year echoes the one we delivered in 1993:  Though we=20
have a fine insurance business, it is not as good as it currently=20
looks.

     The reason we must repeat this caution is that our "super-cat"=20
business (which sells policies that insurance and reinsurance=20
companies buy to protect themselves from the effects of mega-
catastrophes) was again highly profitable.  Since truly major=20
catastrophes occur infrequently, our super-cat business can be=20
expected to show large profits in most years but occasionally to=20
record a huge loss.  In other words, the attractiveness of our=20
super-cat business will take many years to measure.  Certainly 1994=20
should be regarded as close to a best-case.  Our only significant=20
losses arose from the California earthquake in January.  I will add=20
that we do not expect to suffer a major loss from the early-1995=20
Kobe earthquake.

     Super-cat policies are small in number, large in size and non-
standardized.  Therefore, the underwriting of this business=20
requires far more judgment than, say, the underwriting of auto=20
policies, for which a mass of data is available.  Here Berkshire=20
has a major advantage:  Ajit Jain, our super-cat manager, whose=20
underwriting skills are the finest.  His value to us is simply=20
enormous.

     In addition, Berkshire has a special advantage in the super-
cat business because of our towering financial strength, which=20
helps us in two ways.  First, a prudent insurer will want its=20
protection against true mega-catastrophes - such as a $50 billion=20
windstorm loss on Long Island or an earthquake of similar cost in=20
California - to be absolutely certain.  But that same insurer knows=20
that the disaster making it dependent on a large super-cat recovery=20
is also the disaster that could cause many reinsurers to default. =20
There's not much sense in paying premiums for coverage that will=20
evaporate precisely when it is needed.  So the certainty that=20
Berkshire will be both solvent and liquid after a catastrophe of=20
unthinkable proportions is a major competitive advantage for us.

     The second benefit of our capital strength is that we can=20
write policies for amounts that no one else can even consider.  For=20
example, during 1994, a primary insurer wished to buy a short-term=20
policy for $400 million of California earthquake coverage and we=20
wrote the policy immediately.  We know of no one else in the world=20
who would take a $400 million risk, or anything close to it, for=20
their own account.

     Generally, brokers attempt to place coverage for large amounts=20
by spreading the burden over a number of small policies.  But, at=20
best, coverage of that sort takes considerable time to arrange.  In=20
the meantime, the company desiring reinsurance is left holding a=20
risk it doesn't want and that may seriously threaten its well-
being.  At Berkshire, on the other hand, we will quote prices for=20
coverage as great as $500 million on the same day that we are asked=20
to bid.  No one else in the industry will do the same.

     By writing coverages in large lumps, we obviously expose=20
Berkshire to lumpy financial results.  That's totally acceptable to=20
us:  Too often, insurers (as well as other businesses) follow sub-
optimum strategies in order to "smooth" their reported earnings. =20
By accepting the prospect of volatility, we expect to earn higher=20
long-term returns than we would by pursuing predictability.

     Given the risks we accept, Ajit and I constantly focus on our=20
"worst case," knowing, of course, that it is difficult to judge=20
what this is, since you could conceivably have a Long Island=20
hurricane, a California earthquake, and Super Cat X all in the same=20
year.  Additionally, insurance losses could be accompanied by non-
insurance troubles.  For example, were we to have super-cat losses=20
from a large Southern California earthquake, they might well be=20
accompanied by a major drop in the value of our holdings in See's,=20
Wells Fargo and Freddie Mac.

     All things considered, we believe our worst-case <i>insurance</i>=20
loss from a super-cat is now about $600 million after-tax, an=20
amount that would slightly exceed Berkshire's annual earnings from=20
other sources.  If you are not comfortable with this level of=20
exposure, the time to sell your Berkshire stock is now, not after=20
the inevitable mega-catastrophe.

     Our super-cat volume will probably be down in 1995.  Prices=20
for garden-variety policies have fallen somewhat, and the torrent=20
of capital that was committed to the reinsurance business a few=20
years ago will be inclined to chase premiums, irrespective of their=20
adequacy.  Nevertheless, we have strong relations with an important=20
group of clients who will provide us with a substantial amount of=20
business in 1995.

     Berkshire's other insurance operations had excellent results=20
in 1994.  Our homestate operation, led by Rod Eldred; our workers'=20
compensation business, headed by Brad Kinstler; our credit card=20
operation, managed by the Kizer family; National Indemnity's=20
traditional auto and general liability business, led by Don Wurster=20
- all of these generated significant underwriting profits=20
accompanied by substantial float.

     We can conclude this section as we did last year:  All in all,=20
we have a first-class insurance business.  Though its results will=20
be highly volatile, this operation possesses an intrinsic value=20
that exceeds its book value by a large amount - larger, in fact,=20
than is the case at any other Berkshire business.

