     Our investments continue to be few in number and simple in=20
concept:  The truly big investment idea can usually be explained in=20
a short paragraph.  We like a business with enduring competitive=20
advantages that is run by able and owner-oriented people.  When=20
these attributes exist, and when we can make purchases at sensible=20
prices, it is hard to go wrong (a challenge we periodically manage=20
to overcome).

     Investors should remember that their scorecard is not computed=20
using Olympic-diving methods:  Degree-of-difficulty doesn't count.=20
If you are right about a business whose value is largely dependent=20
on a single key factor that is both easy to understand and=20
enduring, the payoff is the same as if you had correctly analyzed=20
an investment alternative characterized by many constantly shifting=20
and complex variables.

     We try to <i>price,</i> rather than <i>time,</i> purchases.  In our vi=
ew, it=20
is folly to forego buying shares in an outstanding business whose=20
long-term future is predictable, because of short-term worries=20
about an economy or a stock market that we know to be=20
unpredictable.  Why scrap an informed decision because of an=20
uninformed guess?

     We purchased National Indemnity in 1967, See's in 1972,=20
Buffalo News in 1977, Nebraska Furniture Mart in 1983, and Scott=20
Fetzer in 1986 because those are the years they became available=20
and because we thought the prices they carried were acceptable.  In=20
each case, we pondered what the business was likely to do, not what=20
the Dow, the Fed, or the economy might do.  If we see this approach=20
as making sense in the purchase of businesses in their entirety,=20
why should we change tack when we are purchasing small pieces of=20
wonderful businesses in the stock market?

     Before looking at new investments, we consider adding to old=20
ones.  If a business is attractive enough to buy once, it may well=20
pay to repeat the process.  We would love to increase our economic=20
interest in See's or Scott Fetzer, but we haven't found a way to=20
add to a 100% holding.  In the stock market, however, an investor=20
frequently gets the chance to increase his economic interest in=20
businesses he knows and likes.  Last year we went that direction by=20
enlarging our holdings in Coca-Cola and American Express.

     Our history with American Express goes way back and, in fact,=20
fits the pattern of my pulling current investment decisions out of=20
past associations.  In 1951, for example, GEICO shares comprised=20
70% of my personal portfolio and GEICO was also the first stock I=20
sold - I was then 20 - as a security salesman (the sale was 100=20
shares to my Aunt Alice who, bless her, would have bought anything=20
I suggested).  Twenty-five years later, Berkshire purchased a major=20
stake in GEICO at the time it was threatened with insolvency.  In=20
another instance, that of the Washington Post, about half of my=20
initial investment funds came from delivering the paper in the=20
1940's.  Three decades later Berkshire purchased a large position=20
in the company two years after it went public.  As for Coca-Cola,=20
my first business venture - this was in the 1930's - was buying a=20
six-pack of Coke for 25 cents and selling each bottle for 5 cents. =20
It took only fifty years before I finally got it:  The real money=20
was in the syrup.

     My American Express history includes a couple of episodes:  In=20
the mid-1960's, just after the stock was battered by the company's=20
infamous salad-oil scandal, we put about 40% of Buffett Partnership=20
Ltd.'s capital into the stock - the largest investment the=20
partnership had ever made.  I should add that this commitment gave=20
us over 5% ownership in Amex at a cost of $13 million.  As I write=20
this, we own just under 10%, which has cost us $1.36 billion. =20
(Amex earned $12.5 million in 1964 and $1.4 billion in 1994.)

     My history with Amex's IDS unit, which today contributes about=20
a third of the earnings of the company, goes back even further.  I=20
first purchased stock in IDS in 1953 when it was growing rapidly=20
and selling at a price-earnings ratio of only 3.  (There was a lot=20
of low-hanging fruit in those days.)  I even produced a long report=20
- do I ever write a short one? - on the company that I sold for $1=20
through an ad in the Wall Street Journal.

     Obviously American Express and IDS (recently renamed American=20
Express Financial Advisors) are far different operations today from=20
what they were then.  Nevertheless, I find that a long-term=20
familiarity with a company and its products is often helpful in=20
evaluating it.

<b>Mistake Du Jour</b>

     Mistakes occur at the time of decision.  We can only make our=20
mistake-du-jour award, however, when the foolishness of the=20
decision become obvious.  By this measure, 1994 was a vintage year=20
with keen competition for the gold medal.  Here, I would like to=20
tell you that the mistakes I will describe originated with Charlie.=20
But whenever I try to explain things that way, my nose begins to=20
grow.

     And the nominees are . . .

     Late in 1993 I sold 10 million shares of Cap Cities at $63; at=20
year-end 1994, the price was $85.25.  (The difference is $222.5=20
million for those of you who wish to avoid the pain of calculating=20
the damage yourself.)  When we purchased the stock at $17.25 in=20
1986, I told you that I had previously sold our Cap Cities holdings=20
at $4.30 per share during 1978-80, and added that I was at a loss=20
to explain my earlier behavior.  Now I've become a repeat offender.=20
Maybe it's time to get a guardian appointed.

     Egregious as it is, the Cap Cities decision earns only a=20
silver medal.  Top honors go to a mistake I made five years ago=20
that fully ripened in 1994:  Our $358 million purchase of USAir=20
preferred stock, on which the dividend was suspended in September.=20
In the 1990 Annual Report I correctly described this deal as an=20
"unforced error," meaning that I was neither pushed into the=20
investment nor misled by anyone when making it.  Rather, this was a=20
case of sloppy analysis, a lapse that may have been caused by the=20
fact that we were buying a senior security or by hubris.  Whatever=20
the reason, the mistake was large.

     Before this purchase, I simply failed to focus on the problems=20
that would inevitably beset a carrier whose costs were both high=20
and extremely difficult to lower.  In earlier years, these life-
threatening costs posed few problems.  Airlines were then protected=20
from competition by regulation, and carriers could absorb high=20
costs because they could pass them along by way of fares that were=20
also high.

     When deregulation came along, it did not immediately change=20
the picture:  The capacity of low-cost carriers was so small that=20
the high-cost lines could, in large part, maintain their existing=20
fare structures.  During this period, with the longer-term problems=20
largely invisible but slowly metastasizing, the costs that were=20
non-sustainable became further embedded.

     As the seat capacity of the low-cost operators expanded, their=20
fares began to force the old-line, high-cost airlines to cut their=20
own.  The day of reckoning for these airlines could be delayed by=20
infusions of capital (such as ours into USAir), but eventually a=20
fundamental rule of economics prevailed:  In an unregulated=20
commodity business, a company must lower its costs to competitive=20
levels or face extinction.  This principle should have been obvious=20
to your Chairman, but I missed it.

     Seth Schofield, CEO of USAir, has worked diligently to correct=20
the company's historical cost problems but, to date, has not=20
managed to do so.  In part, this is because he has had to deal with=20
a moving target, the result of certain major carriers having=20
obtained labor concessions and other carriers having benefitted=20
from "fresh-start" costs that came out of bankruptcy proceedings. =20
(As Herb Kelleher, CEO of Southwest Airlines, has said: =20
"Bankruptcy court for airlines has become a health spa.") =20
Additionally, it should be no surprise to anyone that those airline=20
employees who contractually receive above-market salaries will=20
resist any reduction in these as long as their checks continue to=20
clear.

     Despite this difficult situation, USAir may yet achieve the=20
cost reductions it needs to maintain its viability  long-term.  But=20
it is far from sure that will happen.

     Accordingly, we wrote our USAir investment down to $89.5=20
million, 25 cents on the dollar at yearend 1994.  This valuation=20
reflects both a possibility that our preferred will have its value=20
fully or largely restored and an opposite possibility that the=20
stock will eventually become worthless.  Whatever the outcome, we=20
will heed a prime rule of investing:  You don't have to make it=20
back the way that you lost it.

     The accounting effects of our USAir writedown are complicated.=20
Under GAAP accounting, insurance companies are required to carry=20
all stocks on their balance sheets at estimated market value. =20
Therefore, at the end of last year's third quarter, we were=20
carrying our USAir preferred at $89.5 million, or 25% of cost.  In=20
other words, our net worth was at that time reflecting a value for=20
USAir that was far below our $358 million cost.

     But in the fourth quarter, we concluded that the decline in=20
value was, in accounting terms, "other than temporary," and that=20
judgment required us to send the writedown of $269 million through=20
our income statement.  The amount will have no other fourth-quarter=20
effect.  That is, it will not reduce our net worth, because the=20
diminution of value had already been reflected.

     Charlie and I will not stand for reelection to USAir's board=20
at the upcoming annual meeting.  Should Seth wish to consult with=20
us, however, we will be pleased to be of any help that we can.

<b>Miscellaneous</b>

     Two CEO's who have done great things for Berkshire=20
shareholders retired last year:  Dan Burke of Capital Cities/ABC=20
and Carl Reichardt of Wells Fargo.  Dan and Carl encountered very=20
tough industry conditions in recent years.  But their skill as=20
managers allowed the businesses they ran to emerge from these=20
